  "articleBody": "It’s a common misconception that if you can’t see fire damage, it’s not there. But when your home experiences a fire, even a seemingly small one, the damage is often far more extensive than what's immediately visible. This hidden damage can pose significant long-term problems if not properly addressed. First off, smoke and soot are sneaky. They can permeate porous materials like drywall, upholstery, and even the framework behind your walls, leaving behind corrosive residues and persistent odors. These particles can also get into your HVAC system, circulating throughout your entire home. Second, the heat from a fire can weaken structural components, even if there's no direct charring, compromising the safety of your property. Third, the water used to extinguish the fire can lead to hidden moisture issues within walls and floors, creating a breeding ground for mold if not dried out quickly and thoroughly. Finally, the ",
